<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malolos Constitution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Political Constitution of 1899</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malolos">Malolos</a> Constitution was the constitution of the First Philippine Republic, drafted by a revolutionary congress at Barasoain church in Malolos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulacan">Bulacan</a>, from September 1898 and promulgated in January 1899. Drawing on Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> models, it created a republic with a strong legislature. It was the first republican constitution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, though the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> soon ended the republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malolos-constitution.jpg" alt="Malolos Constitution" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Aguinaldo_(seated,_center)_and_ten_of_the_delegates_to_the_first_Assembly_of_Representatives_that_passed_the_Con_-_NARA_-_530993.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manila-bay">Battle of Manila Bay</a> on 1 May 1898, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo">Emilio Aguinaldo</a> back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. He declared independence on 12 June, and elections held from 23 June to 10 September chose the Malolos Congress, which met on 15 September and named a drafting committee of wealthy, well-educated men.</p><p>The text adopted was written by Felipe Calderón y Roca and Felipe Buencamino as an alternative to proposals by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apolinario-mabini">Apolinario Mabini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-paterno">Pedro Paterno</a>. Congress debated Calderón's draft between 25 October and 29 November 1898, adding a guarantee of religious freedom by a single vote on 28 November. Mabini, Aguinaldo's adviser, thought the system unworkable in wartime, and at Aguinaldo's request provisions were added giving him temporary powers to rule by decree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Written in Spanish, the constitution has 101 articles in fourteen titles, with eight transitory articles and one unnumbered article. Its structure follows the Spanish Constitution of 1812; Calderón also studied the charters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and the French Constitution of 1793. Its central idea was the retroversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to the people. Title IV's twenty-seven articles set out civil liberties, many drawn from the Spanish Constitution of 1869, and Article 5 recognised the freedom of all beliefs and the separation of Church and State.</p><p>The government was to be popular, representative, alternative and responsible, with separate legislative, executive and judicial branches. A one-house National Assembly could censure and question officials, and the president governed through secretaries collectively responsible to it, as in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a>. A Permanent Commission of seven members acted for the Assembly between sessions. Article 99 allowed the executive a free hand in wartime.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>No foreign government recognised the republic, and the constitution was never enforced across the whole archipelago. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-american-war">Philippine–American War</a> began on 4 February 1899, Aguinaldo was captured on 23 March 1901, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-malvar">Miguel Malvar</a> surrendered on 13 April 1902. Later Philippine constitutions, from the Philippine Organic Act of 1902 to the 1987 Constitution, followed American models, so the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malolos">Malolos</a> text had limited influence on them. Isagani Giron, a former president of the Historical Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulacan">Bulacan</a>, called it the best constitution the country ever had. The original is kept in the archives of the Batasang Pambansa Complex, closed to the public, and a marker was placed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barasoain-church">Barasoain Church</a> in 1969.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malolos Constitution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Following the Revolutionary Government of the Philippines and the drafting of the <b>Malolos Constitution</b> by a congress of revolutionary delegates, the Philippine Republic was inaugurated at Barasoain church in Malolos on 23 January 1899, with Emilio Aguinaldo as president and Apolinario Mabini as head of the cabinet.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-philippine-republic">First Philippine Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>A congress met at Barasoain church in Malolos in September 1898 and drafted the <b>Malolos Constitution</b>, drawing on Spanish and Latin American models.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-government-of-the-philippines">Revolutionary Government of the Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
